  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Very little change from yesterday\u2019s \nforecast as upper level ridging to the east and troughing to the \nwest continue to funnel moisture into the Great Basin area. This \nwill result in continued rounds of showers and thunderstorms, \nmainly in central and eastern Nevada today before expanding to the\nentire forecast area tomorrow. Tomorrow is also expected to be \nthe wettest day, with widespread accumulations of 0.25\u201d and a band\nof 0.5\u201d to 0.75\u201d shaping up just south of the US-50 corridor. \nThis has resulted in a Slight (15%) risk of excessive rainfall for\ntomorrow and isolated flash flooding is expected with the \nstrongest cells. The trough begins to lift to the north on Friday,\nweakening the moisture flow for the weekend and mostly cutting \noff storms, leaving about a 10% to 20% chance through Sunday. \nModels remain in disagreement after Sunday, with the biggest \nquestion being the future of the current monsoon event. The \ngeneral indication is still for the monsoon to continue in central\nNevada, however the strength and northern extent of the event \nstill depend on how the upper ridge behaves over the next few days\nand into next week. The current forecast is reflecting the more \ncut off scenario, with dry conditions Monday and Tuesday and some \nisolated showers for Wednesday.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in showers and\nthunderstorms through Friday, with moderate confidence in spatial\nextent of the heaviest accumulations. Moderate confidence in a\nmostly dry weekend. Low confidence in the forecast after Sunday as\nmodels continue to strongly disagree regarding the upper level\npattern. No changes to NBM forecast.",
